\n- F4: North Wind, my seat wind \u2192 if I get a pair, it\u2019s just a pair, no extra points unless also Prevalent wind (East Wind is prevailing, so no).  \n- J2: Green Dragon \u2192 Dragon pair gives no points, but Dragon pung gives points.  \n\nBut I only have 1 each, so likely both are useless unless I draw more.  \n\nBetter plan: discard one and hope to draw a tile that pairs with the other, or draw a tile that can form a chow with something? But with only two singles, chow impossible with honors.  \n\nActually, maybe I should think: maybe my hand could be rearranged: What if I break one of the pungs to form a chow? But T7 pung: I have three T7, no T6 or T8 in hand (T6: 2 left in wall, T8: 0 left), so T7 chow (T567) possible if I draw T6 and T8, unlikely (T8 gone). B6 pung: to make B456 or B678 chow, need B5/B7 or B7/B8 \u2014 B7: 2 left, B5: 3 left, B8: 1 left.  \n\nGiven the unseen tiles, more B5 and B7 left than F4/J2.  \n\nBut breaking a pung for chow is risky.  \n\nBetter: I discard one honor and hope to draw a matching honor for the other, or draw a suited tile that pairs with something else? But I have no other singles.  \n\nGiven tile counts: F4: 3 left, J2: 1 left \u2014 so F4 has more chance to draw for a pair.  \n\nSo I should keep F4, discard J2.  \n\nAlso, if I discard J2, maybe I can later get a replacement tile from kong? Not likely.  \n\nBut also consider defense: other players\u2019 melds show many winds/dragons gone. Previous player discarded J2 earlier, so J2 might be safer to discard.  \n\n**Final choice:** Discard J2 because:  \n1. Lower chance to pair (only 1 left in wall vs F4\u2019s 3 left).  \n2. Less likely to give someone a win? J2 is Green Dragon, but no one has shown J2 meld.  \n3. Keeping F4 gives possible Seat Wind pung if I draw two more (unlikely but possible).  \n\n**Reason:** I need a pair to win; keeping F4 gives better drawing odds for a pair; discarding J2 minimizes loss of potential points and is relatively safe.  \n\n**